QB.add(67,[{"n":1,"q":"A patient presents with joint swelling, a pan systolic murmur, negative rheumatoid factor (RF), and elevated ESR. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Acute rheumatic fever"},{"t":"Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis (RA)"},{"t":"Juvenile spondyloarthropathy"},{"t":"Ankylosing spondylitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Acute rheumatic fever:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF): ARF is an inflammatory disease that can develop after a Streptococcus pyogenes infection (such as strep throat). It commonly presents with migratory arthritis (joint swelling), carditis (which can present as a pan-systolic murmur), and other systemic symptoms. The elevated ESR indicates inflammation, and the negative rheumatoid factor helps distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARF is an inflammatory disease that can develop after a Streptococcus pyogenes infection (such as strep throat). It commonly presents with migratory arthritis (joint swelling), carditis (which can present as a pan-systolic murmur), and other systemic symptoms. The elevated ESR indicates inflammation, and the negative rheumatoid factor helps distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Joint Swelling and Murmur: The presence of joint swelling and a pan-systolic murmur (suggestive of mitral regurgitation due to carditis) is characteristic of ARF. The negative RF and elevated ESR further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of joint swelling and a pan-systolic murmur (suggestive of mitral regurgitation due to carditis) is characteristic of ARF. The negative RF and elevated ESR further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARF is an inflammatory disease that can develop after a Streptococcus pyogenes infection (such as strep throat). It commonly presents with migratory arthritis (joint swelling), carditis (which can present as a pan-systolic murmur), and other systemic symptoms. The elevated ESR indicates inflammation, and the negative rheumatoid factor helps distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ARF is an inflammatory disease that can develop after a Streptococcus pyogenes infection (such as strep throat). It commonly presents with migratory arthritis (joint swelling), carditis (which can present as a pan-systolic murmur), and other systemic symptoms. The elevated ESR indicates inflammation, and the negative rheumatoid factor helps distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Joint Swelling and Murmur:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of joint swelling and a pan-systolic murmur (suggestive of mitral regurgitation due to carditis) is characteristic of ARF. The negative RF and elevated ESR further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of joint swelling and a pan-systolic murmur (suggestive of mitral regurgitation due to carditis) is characteristic of ARF. The negative RF and elevated ESR further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis (RA): Although RA can present with joint swelling and an elevated ESR, a pan-systolic murmur is not typical of RA. Additionally, RA would usually involve symmetrical joint involvement and is less likely to cause heart murmurs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Juvenile spondyloarthropathy: This condition typically affects the spine and sacroiliac joints, not typically presenting with a pan-systolic murmur or significant joint swelling in the way described."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ankylosing spondylitis: Ankylosing spondylitis primarily affects the axial skeleton and does not usually present with joint swelling or a pan-systolic murmur."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is not true about ankylosing spondylitis?","o":[{"t":"Affect males more than females"},{"t":"HLA B27 associated"},{"t":"Involvement of sacroiliac joints"},{"t":"Its non erosive and only joints are affected"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Its non erosive and only joints are affected:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ankylosing spondylitis involves erosive arthritis, where the vertebrae can be affected, leading to erosion at their margins. Also, it is the spine that is affected leading to the fusion of vertebrae and not the joints."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Affect males more than females: Ankylosing spondylitis is more common in males than females contrary to rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - HLA B27 associated: 85-90% shows HLA positivity in ankylosing spondylitis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Involvement of sacroiliac joints: The Sacroiliac joint is the first joint to be involved in ankylosing spondylitis"}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 38-year-old male patient complains of paranasal sinus pain and bloody nasal discharge over the past few days. He has a history of previous hemoptysis and hematuria. Imaging studies reveal multiple bilateral nodular cavitary infiltrates. Which of the following antibodies is likely to be positive in this patient?","o":[{"t":"c-ANCA"},{"t":"ANA"},{"t":"Anti-histone antibodies"},{"t":"Anti-Jo 1 antibodies"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - c-ANCA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These antibodies are associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis), a vasculitis that can involve multiple organs, including the respiratory tract (paranasal sinus involvement), lungs (nodular cavitary infiltrates), and kidneys (hematuria)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - ANA: ANA (antinuclear antibodies) is more commonly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anti-histone antibodies: Anti-histone antibodies are associated with drug-induced lupus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Anti-Jo 1 antibodies: Anti-Jo 1 antibodies are associated with polymyositis and dermatomyositis."}]},{"n":4,"q":"40 year lady presents with difficulty in eating due to persistent dry mouth. She also has grittiness in eyes due to dry eyes. On examination, dental caries is noted with enlarged cervical lymph nodes. Possible diagnosis is:","o":[{"t":"MCTD"},{"t":"Sarcoidosis"},{"t":"Sjogren syndrome"},{"t":"Wegener’s granulomatosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Sjogren syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sjogren syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the salivary and lacrimal glands, leading to dry mouth (xerostomia) and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca). Dental caries are common due to the lack of saliva's protective effects. Enlarged cervical lymph nodes can occur due to immune system activity. This syndrome often presents with these classic symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - MCTD (Mixed Connective Tissue Disease): MCTD is an autoimmune disorder that shares features of multiple other connective tissue diseases, such as systemic lupus erythematosus, scleroderma, and polymyositis. While dry mouth and dry eyes can occur in autoimmune disorders, the specific symptoms described (grittiness in eyes, dental caries, and enlarged cervical lymph nodes) are more characteristic of Sjogren syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sarcoidosis: Sarcoidosis is a disease characterized by the growth of tiny collections of inflammatory cells (granulomas) in different parts of the body. While it can affect various organs, including the lungs, skin, and eyes, the primary symptoms described (dry mouth, dry eyes, dental caries, and enlarged cervical lymph nodes) are not typically associated with sarcoidosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis would more likely involve lung issues and skin lesions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Wegener's Granulomatosis (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis): Wegener's granulomatosis is a type of vasculitis that primarily affects the respiratory tract and kidneys. It usually presents with symptoms related to those organs, such as sinusitis, lung problems, and kidney dysfunction. The symptoms mentioned in the scenario (dry mouth, dry eyes, dental caries, and enlarged cervical lymph nodes) are not typical of Wegener's granulomatosis."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A known RA patient presented to ER with hypotension. She was on medication for RA treatment but abruptly stopped medications. HR =110 bpm BP is 80/40 mm Hg, blood sugar =50 mg/dl, Na=130 meq/L and K=6.0 meq/L. What should be the most appropriate management?","o":[{"t":"Fast IV fluids"},{"t":"Hydrocortisone"},{"t":"Dopamine infusion"},{"t":"Soda bicarbonate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B – Hydrocortisone:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation is of Addisonian crisis that is managed with hydrocortisone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Blood pressure, low Na+ and increased K+ is due to Low aldosterone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypoglycemia of patients is due to low cortisol."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All this suggests an addisonian crisis and this will be managed by Hydrocortisone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An Addisonian crisis, or adrenal crisis, is a potentially life-threatening condition that results from an acute insufficiency of adrenal hormones (glucocorticoid or mineralocorticoid) and requires immediate treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This will stabilize Low BP and Hypoglycemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Fast IV fluids:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Dopamine infusion:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Soda bicarbonate:"}]},{"n":6,"q":"40 yr old woman presents with bilateral symmetrical tender joints of hands, and ankles. She complains of early morning stiffness. On examination, no erythema or redness was found. What could be the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Osteoarthritis"},{"t":"Psoriatic Arthritis"},{"t":"Gout"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Rheumatoid arthritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given clinical picture is suggestive of a diagnosis of Rheumatoid arthritis. Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease characterized by bilateral symmetrical tender joints along with early morning stiffness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A – Osteoarthritis: This option is incorrect as Osteoarthritis typically involves the knee joint and patients are usually between 50 - 60 years of age."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Psoriatic Arthritis: Psoriatic arthritis is an autoimmune disorder with a similar presentation. This condition usually involves fewer joints and the distal interphalangeal joints are spared. Patients. also present skin findings of psoriasis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C – Gout: Gout is a disease caused due to high levels of uric acid in the blood. Men are more commonly affected. The involved joints are usually tender and erythematous."}]},{"n":7,"q":"What extra-articular manifestation is commonly observed in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":[{"t":"Subcutaneous nodule"},{"t":"Sjogren’s syndrome"},{"t":"Felty’s syndrome"},{"t":"Vasculitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Subcutaneous nodule:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subcutaneous nodules are the most common extra-articular manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These nodules typically develop over bony prominences or pressure points and are often found on the extensor surfaces of the forearms and elbows."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sjogren's syndrome: While Sjogren's syndrome, an autoimmune disorder characterized by dry eyes and dry mouth, can occur in patients with rheumatoid arthritis, it is not the most common extra-articular manifestation of the disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Felty's syndrome: Felty's syndrome is a rare complication of rheumatoid arthritis characterized by the triad of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia . It occurs in a small percentage of patients with rheumatoid arthritis but is not the most common extra-articular manifestation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Vasculitis: Vasculitis, inflammation of blood vessels, can occur in rheumatoid arthritis but is not the most common extra-articular manifestation. It is less common compared to subcutaneous nodules."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A female patient presents with fever, oral ulcer, photosensitivity, and rashes on her face as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Dermatomyositis"},{"t":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"},{"t":"Rosacea"},{"t":"Melasma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Systemic lupus erythematosus :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SLE is a chronic autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organs and systems , including the skin, joints, kidneys, heart, and lungs. The butterfly rash, also known as malar rash, is a hallmark feature of SLE and typically appears on the face over the cheeks and bridge of the nose."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[9066]},{"n":9,"q":"What is the next recommended investigation to aid in diagnosing the condition of a patient who has cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy?","o":[{"t":"ANCA"},{"t":"RA factor"},{"t":"HbsAg"},{"t":"MIF"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - ANCA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation of cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy is suggestive of small vessel vasculitis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ANCA testing is a valuable investigation in such case."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ANCA is a group of autoantibodies that target neutrophil cytoplasmic antigens."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Two main patterns of ANCA are recognized: cytoplasmic (c-ANCA) and perinuclear (p-ANCA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"c-ANCA i s typically associated with antibodies against proteinase 3 (PR3) and is seen in granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly known as Wegener's granulomatosis)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"p-ANCA is associated with antibodies against myeloperoxidase (MPO) and is seen in microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly known as Churg-Strauss syndrome)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - RA factor (rheumatoid factor): Rheumatoid factor is an autoantibody commonly associated with rheumatoid arthritis (RA). While RA can involve multiple systems, including joints, it is not typically associated with cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheralneuropathy. Therefore, performing RA factor testing is unlikely to provide relevant diagnosticinformation in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - HbsAg (hepatitis B surface antigen): Hepatitis B infection can present with various clinical manifestations, but cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy are not commonly associated with hepatitis B infection. Therefore, testing for HbsAg is unlikely to help diagnose the underlying condition in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - MIF (migration inhibitory factor): Migration inhibitory factor is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that has been implicated in various inflammatory conditions. However, it is not specific to any particular disease or diagnostic entity. Testing for MIF alone would not provide sufficient diagnostic information to identify the underlying cause of the patient's presentation with cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy."}]},{"n":10,"q":"Which of the following medications is not used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","o":[{"t":"Febuxostat"},{"t":"Leflunomide"},{"t":"Etanercept"},{"t":"Methotrexate"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Febuxostat:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Febuxostat is not used in the treatment of RA. It is a medication primarily used for the management of hyperuricemia (elevated levels of uric acid) in patients with gout. It works by inhibiting the enzyme xanthine oxidase, which is involved in the production of uric acid. While gout and RA are both inflammatory conditions, the use of febuxostat is not considered a standard treatment for RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are used in the management of patients with rehumatoid arthritis."}]},{"n":11,"q":"An elderly woman presents with a chronic history of pain in the small joints of hands with stiffness of joints in the early hours of the day. The image of the patient's hands is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis"},{"t":"Osteoarthritis"},{"t":"Complex Regional Pain Syndrome"},{"t":"Villonodular synovitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Rheumatoid Arthritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given scenario, the patient is presenting with chronic pain in the small joints of the hands, along with stiffness of the joints in the early hours of the day. The image of the patient's hands is consistent with the characteristic findings seen in Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rheumatoid Arthritis is a chronic autoimmune disease that primarily affects the joints. It typically presents with symmetrical involvement of the small joints, such as those in the hands and wrists. The characteristic features of RA include joint pain, swelling, and morning stiffness that lasts for at least one hour. The image provided shows the presence of joint swelling, deformities (such as ulnar deviation and swan neck deformity), and changes in the finger joints, which are commonly seen in RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Osteoarthritis: Osteoarthritis is a degenerative joint disease characterized by the breakdown of joint cartilage. While it can also affect the small joints of the hands, the image and the clinical features described in the scenario are more indicative of Rheumatoid Arthritis. Osteoarthritis also does not present with symmetric involvement of joints unlike RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Complex Regional Pain Syndrome: Complex Regional Pain Syndrome, also known as Reflex Sympathetic Dystrophy, is a condition characterized by chronic pain, swelling, and changes in skin color and temperature in the affected limb. The presentation and image provided in the scenario are not consistent with Complex Regional Pain Syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Villonodular Synovitis: Villonodular Synovitis is a rare condition characterized by the proliferation of synovial tissue in joints, tendon sheaths, or bursae. While it can lead to joint pain and swelling, it typically presents with localized findings and is less likely to involve multiple small joints as seen in the image."}],"qi":[9069]},{"n":12,"q":"A triad of asthma, systemic vasculitis and eosinophilia is seen in which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis"},{"t":"Polyarteritis nodosa"},{"t":"Churg Strauss syndrome"},{"t":"Giant cell arteritis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Churg Strauss Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Churg Strauss syndrome, also known as eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA), is a rare autoimmune condition characterized by a triad of symptoms including asthma, eosinophilia, and systemic vasculitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features of EPGA are: Eosinophilic vasculitis that involves multiple organ systems Asthma Peripheral Eosinophilia Neuropathy Pulmonary infiltrates Paranasal sinus abnormalities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eosinophilic vasculitis that involves multiple organ systems"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asthma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral Eosinophilia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary infiltrates"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paranasal sinus abnormalities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eosinophilic vasculitis that involves multiple organ systems"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asthma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral Eosinophilia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary infiltrates"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paranasal sinus abnormalities"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Common neurological tumour in NF-2?","o":[{"t":"Vestibular Schwannoma"},{"t":"Optic glioma"},{"t":"Café-Au-Lait macules"},{"t":"Neurofibrosarcoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Vestibular Schwannoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral Vestibular Schwannomas are most common tumor seen in patients with neurofibromatosis type 2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Optic glioma: Optic gliomas are tumors that affect the optic nerve. While they can be seen in other conditions such as neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF-1), they are not typically associated with NF-2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Café-Au-Lait macules: Café-au-lait macules are pigmented skin lesions that can be seen in both NF-1 and NF-2. However, they are not tumors and do not specifically indicate the presence of a neurological tumor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Neurofibrosarcoma: Neurofibrosarcoma is associated with neurofibromatosis type 1."}]},{"n":14,"q":"All of the following characteristics are found in the pleural effusion fluid of a rheumatoid arthritis patient except:","o":[{"t":"RA factor"},{"t":"High glucose"},{"t":"Cholesterol crystals"},{"t":"High LDH"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - High glucose:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High glucose levels are typically not a characteristic feature of pleural effusion in rheumatoid arthritis. Elevated glucose levels in pleural fluid are commonly associated with conditions such as bacterial infections or malignancies. Rheumatoid effusion is usually associated with glucose <40 mg/dL."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - RA factor: RA factor (rheumatoid factor) is an autoantibody commonly found in the blood of individuals with rheumatoid arthritis. It can also be detected in the pleural effusion fluid of RA patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cholesterol crystals: Cholesterol crystals can be present in the pleural effusion fluid of rheumatoid arthritis patients. These crystals may form due to inflammation and the breakdown of cellular membranes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - High LDH: High LDH (lactate dehydrogenase) levels are commonly seen in pleural effusions, including those associated with rheumatoid arthritis. LDH is an enzyme released from damaged or inflamed cells, and its elevation in the pleural fluid can indicate inflammation or tissue damage."}]},{"n":15,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis of a 45-year-old woman who complains of dryness in her mouth and eyes, and has positive anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies? The image below shows a positive test result with a score of less than 5 mm in 5 minutes without anesthesia.","o":[{"t":"Scleroderma"},{"t":"Dehydration"},{"t":"Lacrimal duct stones"},{"t":"Sjogren syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Sjogren Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above image is that of Schirmer’s test, and in the above case, the patient, a 45-year-old female patient, presents with dryness in the mouth and eyes. Schirmer’s was positive with a score of <5 mm in 5 minutes without anesthesia, indicating a tear deficiency The patient is also positive for anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies. The likely diagnosis in this case is Sjogren syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Scleroderma: Scleroderma antibodies are anti-Scl-70 and anti-centromere."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B & C - Dehydration & Lacrimal duct stones: Dehydration & Lacrimal duct stones are unlikely since this patient tested positive for anti Ro and anti La antibodies which are characteristic of Sjogren syndrome."}],"qi":[9073]},{"n":16,"q":"A 29-year-old female patient presents with multiple oral and genital ulcers. The patient claims these ulcers resolve spontaneously and that she experiences such episodes at least four times a year. Ocular examination reveals features of anterior uveitis. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Psoriatic arthritis"},{"t":"SLE"},{"t":"Behcet’s disease"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Behcet’s disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Behcet's disease is a chronic autoimmune disorder characterized by recurrent oral and genital ulcers. It is a multi-systemic vasculitis that can affect blood vessels of all sizes. One of the common manifestations of Behcet's disease is uveitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect and the presentation described in the vignette is more characteristic of Behcet's disease."}]},{"n":17,"q":"What is the accurate description of marble Schonberg disease?","o":[{"t":"Osteopetrosis"},{"t":"Osteomalacia"},{"t":"Osteosclerosis"},{"t":"Osteonecrosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Osteopetrosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Osteopetrosis (Marble Bone/Albers Schonberg disease): In osteopetrosis there is a defect in the normal process of bone resorption, resulting in excessive accumulation of dense, brittle bone tissue. This leads to increased bone density and reduced bone marrow space. The term \"marble bone\" refers to the appearance of the bones on imaging studies, which appear dense and resemble marble. Osteopetrosis can be inherited as an autosomal recessive or autosomal dominant condition and can manifest with various symptoms such as fractures, anemia, and impaired vision or hearing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":18,"q":"What is the accurate statement regarding rheumatoid nodules?","o":[{"t":"Tender, located on extensor surface and seen with arthritis"},{"t":"Non tender, located on extensor surface and seen with arthritis"},{"t":"Non tender, located on flexor surface and seen with arthritis"},{"t":"Tender, located on flexor surface and seen with arthritis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Non tender, located on extensor surface and seen with arthritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rheumatoid nodules are subcutaneous nodules characteristic of rheumatoid arthritis. They are usually painless (non-tender) and are typically found over bony prominences, especially on the extensor surfaces of joints, such as the elbows. Rheumatoid nodules result from the accumulation of inflammatory cells and fibrous tissue and are a specific clinical manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 24-year-old female patient presented with malar rash, recurrent oral ulcers, and multiple joint pains. She has no relevant past history and does not take any medication. On further investigations, thrombocytopenia was seen, and the anti-Sm antibody was positive. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Scleroderma"},{"t":"Sjogren’s syndrome"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Systemic lupus erythematosus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is a connective tissue disorder characterized by a malar rash, recurrent oral ulcers, and multiple joint pains. Thrombocytopenia and anti-Sm antibodies also support the diagnosis of systemic lupus erythematosus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":20,"q":"Anti-centromere antibodies are seen in which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"SLE"},{"t":"Drug induced lupus"},{"t":"Sjogren syndrome"},{"t":"Scleroderma"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Scleroderma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies are strongly associated with systemic sclerosis (scleroderma), specifically the limited cutaneous form of the disease known as limited systemic sclerosis or CREST syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These antibodies are found in approximately 60-80% of patients with limited systemic sclerosis/CREST syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies are considered a specific marker for this subtype of systemic sclerosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies are not seen in options A, B, and C."}]},{"n":21,"q":"What is the antibody linked to the condition in which a 35-year-old woman experiences skin thickening, muscle weakness, pale peripheries upon cold exposure, increased creatine kinase with positive ANA, and biopsy revealing scl-70 positivity and perifascicular infiltration?","o":[{"t":"Anti PM scl antibody"},{"t":"Anti Jo1 antibody"},{"t":"Anti centromere antibody"},{"t":"Antinuclear antibody"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Anti PM scl antibody:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis in this patient with skin thickening, scl positivity, and perifascicular infiltration is overlap syndrome. This overlap syndrome is characteristic of systemic sclerosis + Dermatomyositis/Polymyositis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The characteristic antibody in this scenario is Anti PM scl antibody."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":22,"q":"Which is the most specific antibody in systemic sclerosis?","o":[{"t":"Anti-DNA"},{"t":"Anti-topoisomerase"},{"t":"ANA"},{"t":"Anti-Ro"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Anti-topoisomerase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-topoisomerase (Anti-Scl-70) antibodies are highly specific for systemic sclerosis, particularly its diffuse cutaneous subtype."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These antibodies target an enzyme called topoisomerase I, which is involved in DNA replication and repair."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of anti-topoisomerase antibodies is strongly associated with skin involvement and internal organ complications in systemic sclerosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Anti-DNA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-DNA antibodies are typically associated with autoimmune diseases such as systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) rather than systemic sclerosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While anti-DNA antibodies may be present in some cases of systemic sclerosis, they are not considered specific to the disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - ANA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ANA (antinuclear antibodies) is a general term for antibodies that target components within the cell nucleus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While ANA can be detected in various autoimmune diseases, including systemic sclerosis, it is not specific to the condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ANA testing is often used as a screening tool to indicate the presence of an autoimmune process, but it does not provide a definitive diagnosis on its own."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Anti-Ro:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-Ro (SS-A) antibodies are associated with various autoimmune conditions, including systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and Sjögren's syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While they may occasionally be detected in systemic sclerosis, they are not specific to the disease."}]},{"n":23,"q":"Preferred drug in the management of a patient with migratory polyarthritis and joint pain in acute rheumatic fever?","o":[{"t":"Furosemide"},{"t":"Digoxin"},{"t":"Aspirin"},{"t":"Amiodarone"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Aspirin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The commonest manifestation of rheumatic fever in India is rheumatic arthritis, migratory polyarthritis, and joint pains."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aspirin is mainly used in the treatment of the rheumatic arthritis component. This usually relieves the symptoms of arthritis rapidly and a response within 24 hours helps confirm the diagnosis. A reasonable starting dose is 60 mg/kg body weight/day, divided into six doses. In adults, 100 mg/kg per day may be needed up to the limits of tolerance or a maximum of 8 g per day."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gluccocorticoids produce more rapid symptomatic relief than aspirin and are indicated in cases with carditis or severe arthritis. There is no evidence that long-term steroids are beneficial."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For secondary prevention, Benzathine penicillin is used for the prevention of recurrent sore throats."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gluccocorticoids produce more rapid symptomatic relief than aspirin and are indicated in cases with carditis or severe arthritis. There is no evidence that long-term steroids are beneficial."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Furosemide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide is used in the treatment of heart failure due to ARF"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Digoxin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digoxin may be used in Rheumatoid heart disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Amiodarone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiodarone is used in treating atrial fibrillation in rheumatic heart disease patients."}]},{"n":24,"q":"After confirming a diagnosis of gout, which of the following drugs is a purine analog that would be most appropriate for management?","o":[{"t":"Allopurinol"},{"t":"Capecitabine"},{"t":"Cytarabine"},{"t":"Febuxostat"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A- Allopurinol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Allopurinol is a purine analog and xanthine oxidase inhibitor that reduces the production of uric acid by inhibiting the enzyme xanthine oxidase."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Allopurinol lowers the serum urate by a dual action of inhibiting xanthine oxidase inhibitor as well as by competing with phosphoribosylpyrophosphate in the salvage pathway and by suppressive effects of drug nucleotides on the aminotransferase activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Capecitabine: Capecitabine is a pyrimidine analog, an oral drug approved for the treatment of breast cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C- Cytarabine: Cytarabine is a cytosine arabinoside and a pyrimidine analog used as a chemotherapy agent for various cancers."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- Febuxostat: Febuxostat is also a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, similar to allopurinol. It is not a purine analog. Allopurinol remains the first-line agent due to its long-standing safety profile and lower cost compared to febuxostat."}]},{"n":25,"q":"The slowing of conduction in multiple sclerosis is due to which of the following?","o":[{"t":"Gliosis"},{"t":"Loss of myelin sheath"},{"t":"Defect at node of Ranvier"},{"t":"Leaky Na + channel"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Loss of myelin sheath:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the correct answer. Multiple sclerosis is a chronic autoimmune disorder characterized by the destruction of myelin, the protective covering of nerve fibers in the CNS. The loss of myelin disrupts the normal conduction of nerve impulses, resulting in a slowing of nerve transmission and the characteristic neurological symptoms seen in MS patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C, and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":26,"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis for a young male patient who experiences drooping eyelids that worsens as the day progresses, along with increased fatigue towards the end of the day which improves with rest? Additionally, the patient notices a significant decrease in drooping when placing an ice pack over their eyes.","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Huntington chorea"},{"t":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"},{"t":"External ophthalmoplegia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Myasthenia gravis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder that affects the neuromuscular junction, causing muscle weakness and fatigue. It is caused by antibodies that attack the acetylcholine receptors on the muscle, leading to a decrease in the number of available receptors and impaired nerve transmission. Ptosis, or drooping of the eyelid, is a common symptom. The ptosis tends to worsen through the day as more acetylcholine receptors are involved. Patients may also experience double vision, difficulty speaking or swallowing, and weakness in the limbs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":27,"q":"What is the likely diagnosis for a 50-year-old woman who is experiencing challenges with activities such as climbing stairs, rising from a chair, and grooming her hair, along with the observation of violaceous redness on her upper eyelids?","o":[{"t":"Inclusion body myositis"},{"t":"Dermatomyositis"},{"t":"Polymyositis"},{"t":"Scleroderma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Dermatomyositis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the symptoms described the most likely diagnosis is Dermatomyositis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dermatomyositis is an inflammatory myopathy that causes muscle weakness as well as skin involvement ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It affects both children and adults, with a higher prevalence in middle-aged people."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dermatomyositis causes muscle weakness in the proximal muscles and can also affect the muscles of the neck and throat, making swallowing or speaking difficult."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Inclusion body myositis: Inclusion body myositis is an inflammatory muscle disorder that causes muscle weakness and wasting. However, it usually affects older peopleand is associated with distal muscle weakness (e.g., finger flexors, quadriceps)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Polymyositis: Polymyositis is an autoimmune inflammatory muscle disease that causes muscle weakness in the proximal muscles. It is not commonly associated with the skin manifestations seen in dermatomyositis, such as violaceous erythema on the upper eyelids."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Scleroderma: Scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis, is a chronic autoimmune connective tissue disorder affecting the skin and various internal organs. While it can cause muscle weakness, it does not usually present with the typical skin changes seen in dermatomyositis, such as violaceous erythema of the upper eyelids. Skin thickening and fibrosis, involvement of multiple organ systems, and the presence of specific autoantibodies are all symptoms of scleroderma."}]},{"n":28,"q":"A young man came to the medical OPD with complaints of early morning backache and stiffness, which improves on exercise, and persistent red eyes. On examination, lung expansion was less than 3 cm. X-ray is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Ankylosing spondylitis"},{"t":"Paget's disease"},{"t":"Healed tuberculosis"},{"t":"Osteopetrosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Ankylosing spondylitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In X-ray, the appearance of the Bamboo spine is seen, which is characteristic of Ankylosing spondylitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low backache and nocturnal pain that decreases with activity is also suggestive of Ankylosing spondylitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ankylosing spondylitis is an inflammatory disease of the axial spine. It begins in the early years of life when patients present with stiffness and pain in the spine, which gets relieved as the day progresses. The inflammation can cause fusion of the bones, thus leading to the appearance of the “bamboo spine”, as seen in the patient’s X-ray. If the fusion occurs at the thoracic vertebra level, it can lead to difficulty in chest movement, causing decreased chest expansion, as seen in the patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B Paget's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While Paget's disease can affect the spine, causing deformities and pain, it typically does not present with persistent red eyes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, lung expansion would not typically be affected in Paget's disease unless there are associated complications such as vertebral compression leading to restrictive lung disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"option c: Healed tuberculosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Healed tuberculosis may present with backache and stiffness, especially if there was previous involvement of the spine (Pott's disease)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, persistent red eyes and decreased lung expansion are not typical features of healed tuberculosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray findings of a bamboo spine, which is characteristic of ankylosing spondylitis, do not align with healed tuberculosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"option d: Osteopetrosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Osteopetrosis, also known as marble bone disease, is a rare genetic disorder characterized by abnormal bone density and structure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While osteopetrosis can lead to bone pain and stiffness, it typically does not present with early morning backache and stiffness that improves with exercise."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Persistent red eyes and a bamboo spine on X-ray are not features of osteopetrosis."}],"qi":[9082]},{"n":29,"q":"A chronic alcoholic patient presents with acute pain and swelling of the left great toe. There is no history of trauma. Synovial fluid analysis shows elevated1 leukocytes. Lab investigations show normal serum uric acid levels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pseudogout"},{"t":"Acute gout"},{"t":"Reactive arthritis"},{"t":"Septic arthritis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Acute gout:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute gout is a form of arthritis is characterized by severe pain, redness, and tenderness in joints, most often the big toe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When too much uric acid crystallizes and accumulates in the joints, it can result in pain and inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The serum uric acid levels do not need to be raised for the uric acid to crystallize and deposit in the joints, but an increased serum uric acid level does predispose patients to this condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gout attacks can come suddenly and are often at night. Increased consumption of alcohol also facilitates the increased production of uric acid and its decreased excretion in urine, causing its accumulation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thus, the most likely diagnosis for the patient is acute gout."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pseudogout: Pseudogout is a condition that develops when tiny calcium pyrophosphate crystals gather in a joint and cause an immune system reaction. Any joint can have pseudogout. The knee, wrist, or large knuckles of the hand (metacarpophalangeal joints) are the areas most likely to be affected, but they can also impact the hip, shoulder, and spine. Pseudogout, unlike gout, hardly ever affects the big toe."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Reactive arthritis: An infection of the gastrointestinal or genitourinary system can cause reactive arthritis, an inflammatory form of arthritis that develops days to weeks later."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Septic arthritis: Infectious arthritis is another name for septic arthritis, which is typically brought on by bacteria. This condition is an infection-related joint inflammation. Septic arthritis typically impacts one sizable joint, like the knee or hip."}]},{"n":30,"q":"Which of the following is not a first-line drug for the management of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":[{"t":"Sulfasalazine"},{"t":"Hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Methotrexate"},{"t":"Azathioprine"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Azathioprine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine is not considered a first-line drug for the management of rheumatoid arthritis (RA). It is primarily used as an immunosuppressant in organ transplantation and certain autoimmune conditions but is not typically recommended as an initial treatment for RA due to it’s inconsistent efficacy and high toxicity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Sulfasalazine: Sulfasalazine is one of the disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) commonly used in the treatment of RA. It helps reduce inflammation and joint damage and is considered a first-line treatment option for RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hydroxychloroquine: Hydroxychloroquine is another DMARD used in the management of RA. It has anti-inflammatory properties and can help control symptoms and slow disease progression. It is often prescribed as a first-line treatment option, especially in mild to moderate RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Methotrexate: Methotrexate is widely recognized as the first-line DMARD for the treatment of RA. It is highly effective in reducing symptoms, preventing joint damage, and improving long-term outcomes. Methotrexate is considered the anchor drug and is often prescribed as the initial treatment for most patients with RA."}]},{"n":31,"q":"A young patient presents to the clinic with erythematous lesions over the exposed areas of the skin, like hands, arms, and chest. He also complains of arthralgia and breathlessness. Measuring which among the following antibodies will be useful in diagnosing the underlying conditions?","o":[{"t":"Antihistone antibodies"},{"t":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies"},{"t":"Anticentromere antibodies"},{"t":"Antinuclear antibodies"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Anti-dsDNA antibodies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis in this patient is systemic lupus erythematosus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies are specific to systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), a chronic autoimmune disease. These antibodies target double-stranded DNA and are found in a significant number of patients with SLE. Their presence is highly specific to SLE and is associated with lupus nephritis (kidney involvement) and other manifestations of the disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Antihistone antibodies: Antihistone antibodies are antibodies that target histones, proteins associated with DNA in the nucleus. They are most commonly associated with drug-induced lupus, a condition that resembles SLE but is caused by certain medications. In this scenario, where the symptoms are suggestive of SLE, testing for antihistone antibodies would be less relevant."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anticentromere antibodies: Anticentromere antibodies are associated with a distinct autoimmune disease called limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis, also known as CREST syndrome. This condition primarily affects the skin, blood vessels, and internal organs. The symptoms and clinical features described in the patient are not consistent with CREST syndrome, making testing for anticentromere antibodies less useful in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Antinuclear antibodies (ANA): Antinuclear antibodies target components within the cell nucleus and can be found in various autoimmune diseases, including SLE. While antinuclear antibodies are a useful screening test for autoimmune diseases, including SLE, they are not as specific to SLE as anti dsDNA antibodies."}]},{"n":32,"q":"A 36-year-old woman presented with claudication in the forearm, transient loss of vision, and abdominal pain. Femoral pulses were weak. Fundus examination revealed retinal haemorrhages. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Polyarteritis nodosa"},{"t":"Thromboangiitis obliterans"},{"t":"Takayasu arteritis"},{"t":"Microscopic polyangiitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Takayasu arteritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Takayasu arteritis is a large vessel vasculitis that affects the aorta and its major branches. It primarily affects young women and presents with symptoms related to the affected arteries. Common manifestations include weak or absent pulses, claudication, blood pressure differences between arms, and visual disturbances due to ocular artery involvement. The symptoms described in the case, including claudication, weak femoral pulses, transient loss of vision, and retinal hemorrhages, are highly suggestive of Takayasu arteritis. Therefore, Takayasu arteritis is the most likely diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Polyarteritis nodosa: Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis that primarily affects medium-sized arteries. It typically presents with constitutional symptoms such as fever, weight loss, and fatigue. It can involve multiple organ systems, including the skin, joints, kidneys, and gastrointestinal tract. However, the symptoms described in the case, such as claudication, transient loss of vision, and retinal hemorrhages, are not characteristic of polyarteritis nodosa."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Thromboangiitis obliterans: Thromboangiitis obliterans, also known as Buerger's disease, is a non-atherosclerotic segmental inflammatory disease that affects small and medium-sized arteries and veins, particularly in the extremities. It is strongly associated with tobacco smoking. The typical presentation includes claudication, digital ischemia (such as ulcers or gangrene), and Raynaud's phenomenon. While the symptoms of claudication and peripheral arterial involvement are present in the case, the involvement of the eyes (transient loss of vision, retinal hemorrhages) and abdominal pain make thromboangiitis obliterans less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Microscopic polyangiitis: Microscopic polyangiitis is a small vessel vasculitis that primarily affects capillaries, venules, and arterioles. It can involve multiple organ systems, including the kidneys, lungs, skin, and peripheral nerves. While it can present with systemic symptoms and renal involvement, the specific symptoms described in the case, such as claudication, transient loss of vision, and retinal hemorrhages, are not typical of microscopic polyangiitis."}]},{"n":33,"q":"A woman presents with numbness of her fingertips. On examination, the skin over her face appears tightened. The antinuclear antibody (ANA) is found to be positive and immunofluorescence shows the nucleolar pattern. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Systemic sclerosis"},{"t":"Sjogren’s syndrome"},{"t":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Systemic sclerosis (Scleroderma):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic sclerosis is an autoimmune connective tissue disorder characterized by excessive fibrosis and thickening of the skin and internal organs. It can present with Raynaud's phenomenon, which is often the initial symptom and can cause numbness of the fingertips. The tightening of the skin over the face, known as \"scleroderma,\" is a characteristic feature of systemic sclerosis. The presence of a positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) with a nucleolar pattern on immunofluorescence testing is associated with systemic sclerosis. Therefore, systemic sclerosis is the likely diagnosis in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sjogren's syndrome: Sjogren's syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the exocrine glands, leading to dryness of the eyes and mouth."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE): SLE is a multisystem autoimmune disease that can affect various organs and tissues. It can present with a wide range of symptoms, including numbness and tingling in the extremities. However, the tightening of the face seen in systemic sclerosis and the nucleolar pattern on ANA immunofluorescence testing are not characteristic of SLE. Therefore, SLE is less likely to be the diagnosis in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rheumatoid arthritis: Rheumatoid arthritis primarily affects the joints, causing inflammation, pain, and stiffness. It does not typically involve numbness of the fingertips or the tightening of the face seen in systemic sclerosis. Additionally, the nucleolar pattern on ANA immunofluorescence testing is not associated with rheumatoid arthritis. Therefore, rheumatoid arthritis is less likely to be the diagnosis in this case."}]},{"n":34,"q":"What should be the first-line treatment for a patient with giant cell arteritis?","o":[{"t":"Abatacept"},{"t":"Tocilizumab"},{"t":"Steroids"},{"t":"Aspirin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Steroids:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Steroids, This option is the correct answer. Glucocorticoids (steroids) are the first-line treatment for GCA. They have been shown to be highly effective in reducing inflammation and preventing complications associated with GCA. The initial dosage of steroids is usually high, with gradual tapering based on clinical response and laboratory markers of inflammation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":35,"q":"Which among the following is more common in limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis when compared to diffuse systemic sclerosis?","o":[{"t":"Esophageal dysmotility"},{"t":"Myopathy"},{"t":"Interstitial lung disease"},{"t":"Scleroderma renal crisis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Esophageal dysmotility:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis, also known as limited scleroderma or CREST syndrome, esophageal dysmotility is more common compared to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis. Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is characterized by skin involvement limited to the face, neck, and distal extremities, while diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis involves more widespread skin fibrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are more commonly associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis."}]},{"n":36,"q":"Which of the following is true about polyarteritis nodosa?","o":[{"t":"Necrotising inflammation of large vessels"},{"t":"Patient has hypogammaglobulinemia"},{"t":"90% is associated with ANCA positivity"},{"t":"30% is associated with Hepatitis B"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - 30% is associated with Hepatitis B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is true. Approximately 30% of cases of polyarteritis nodosa are associated with chronic Hepatitis B infection. The immune response triggered by the Hepatitis B virus can cause immune complex deposition and subsequent inflammation in the arterial walls, leading to the development of PAN."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":37,"q":"A patient presents to you with fever, night sweats, ptosis, and bilateral facial nerve palsy. Investigations showed leukocytosis and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Sarcoidosis"},{"t":"Tuberculosis"},{"t":"Lymphoma"},{"t":"Hypersensitive pneumonitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Sarcoidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of fever, night sweats, bilateral facial nerve paralysis, and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy are suggestive of a systemic inflammatory condition affecting multiple organs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disorder that commonly presents with hilar lymphadenopathy besides other features of systemic involvement such as facial nerve palsy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Tuberculosis : It can display bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and fever, but it is less likely to cause facial nerve palsy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C and D (Lymphoma & Hypersensitive pneumonitis) are incorrect."}]},{"n":38,"q":"Which drugs are not used for a patient diagnosed with sarcoidosis?","o":[{"t":"Fludrocortisone"},{"t":"Hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Methotrexate"},{"t":"Prednisone"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Fludrocortisone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis is an inflammatory disorder most commonly seen in the lungs. Patients may exhibit symptoms like cough and dyspnoea, which are mostly treated with glucocorticoids. The first-line treatment is glucocorticoids with additional DMARDS like methotrexate, hydroxychloroquine, biologics like TNF alpha inhibitors, and Azathioprine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid mainly used for Addison’s disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are used in the management of patients with sarcoidosis."}]},{"n":39,"q":"A patient presents with severe pain and swelling in his knee joint for 10 days. He also complains of pain and discomfort during urination. He says that he had diarrhea one month ago and he has been unwell since then. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Reactive arthritis"},{"t":"Enteropathic arthritis"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Psoriatic arthritis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Reactive arthritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above case, there is a history of knee swelling, urethritis, and diarrhea. It points towards the diagnosis of reactive arthritis . It is a post-infectious autoimmune disease. The most common systems affected would be the gastrointestinal or urinary tract."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Enteropathic arthritis: Enteropathic arthritis Is a spondyloarthropathy that is associated with inflammatory bowel diseases like Crohn’s and ulcerative colitis. It typically causes sacroiliitis and spondylitis and involves peripheral joints."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Rheumatoid arthritis: Rheumatoid arthritis is seen in mostly young females and presents with painful tender joints. PIP and MCP are affected symmetrically in bilateral limbs. Patients experience morning stiffness and the hallmark feature is flexor tenosynovitis. Swan neck deformity and Boutonnière deformity is also seen."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Psoriatic arthritis: Psoriatic arthritis is associated with a chronic inflammatory skin disorder. Psoriasis causes Inflammatory arthritis and primarily affects the hands, feet, or spine."}]},{"n":40,"q":"Which drug is the preferred choice for managing primary progressive multiple sclerosis?","o":[{"t":"Natalizumab"},{"t":"Ocrelizumab"},{"t":"Fingolimod"},{"t":"Alemtuzumab"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Ocrelizumab:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocrelizumab is an approved medication for the treatment of both relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis (MS) and primary progressive MS. It is a monoclonal antibody that specifically targets CD20-positive B cells, leading to their depletion. Ocrelizumab has shown efficacy in reducing disease activity and slowing disability progression in patients with PPMS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":41,"q":"A patient presented with sinusitis, ulcerative lesions on the nasopharynx, nodular lesions with cavitations of the lung, and renal failure. Which of the following is the most useful investigation for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Test for ANCA"},{"t":"Biopsy to show granuloma"},{"t":"AFB staining of sputum"},{"t":"BAL"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Test for ANCA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The combination of sinusitis, ulcerative lesions on the nasopharynx, nodular lesions with cavitations of the lung, and renal failure raises suspicion for a condition known as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) , formerly called Wegener’s granulomatosis . GPA is a systemic autoimmune vasculitis that primarily affects the small and medium-sized blood vessels, leading to inflammation and tissue damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Testing for ANCA is crucial in evaluating patients with suspected GPA. ANCA, specifically the c-ANCA (cytoplasmic ANCA) subtype, is present in many GPA cases. The presence of c-ANCA, directed against proteinase 3 (PR3) antibodies, supports the diagnosis of GPA. However, it’s important to note that a small percentage of GPA cases may be ANCA-negative."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Biopsy to show granuloma: While granulomas can be seen in GPA, they are not always present, and their absence does not exclude the diagnosis. Biopsy findings can provide supportive evidence but are not definitive on their own."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - AFB staining of sputum: Acid-fast bacilli (AFB) staining is useful for detecting organisms such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which causes tuberculosis. However, the clinical presentation described in the question is not suggestive of tuberculosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - BAL (Bronchoalveolar Lavage): BAL can be helpful in evaluating lung involvement in GPA and identifying inflammatory cells, but it is not the most useful investigation for confirming the diagnosis. ANCA testing provides more specific information in this scenario."}]},{"n":42,"q":"A 52 year old woman presented with changes in the hand as shown below. There is decreased motion in the small joints and loss of wrinkle lines on the facial skin. Her chest radiograph showed prominent bilateral lower lobe infiltrates. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Scleroderma"},{"t":"Systemic lupus Erythematosus"},{"t":"Pneumonia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Scleroderma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation and findings, the most likely diagnosis in this case is Scleroderma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis, is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by fibrosis (hardening) of the skin and internal organs. The image in the vignette depicts Raynaud's phenomenon, which is seen in patients with scleroderma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of wrinkle lines on the facial skin, known as the \"mask-like\" facies, is another clinical manifestation commonly seen in scleroderma. This occurs due to thickening and tightening of the skin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The prominent bilateral lower lobe infiltrates on the chest radiograph suggest pulmonary involvement, which is common in scleroderma. Pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, and pulmonary hypertension are potential complications of scleroderma that can manifest as lung infiltrates."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[9092]},{"n":43,"q":"A 19 year old woman with no comorbidities presented with numbness and paraesthesia of the fingers along with the characteristic finding as shown in the image below. She has no other illnesses and she says these episodes occur when she is under excess stress or during cold climates. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Primary Raynaud’s phenomenon"},{"t":"Secondary Raynaud’s phenomenon"},{"t":"Cold sores"},{"t":"Sclerodema"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Primary Raynaud’s phenomenon:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis for the 19-year-old woman is Primary Raynaud's phenomenon."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Raynaud's phenomenon is characterized by episodic vasospasm of the digital arteries, leading to color changes, numbness, and paraesthesia of the fingers or toes. There are two main types: Primary Raynaud's phenomenon and Secondary Raynaud's phenomenon."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud's phenomenon: This occurs without an underlying cause or associated medical condition. It is usually triggered by cold temperatures or emotional stress. The color changes during an episode include pallor (white discoloration) followed by cyanosis (bluish discoloration) and then reactive hyperemia (redness) as blood flow returns. The characteristic finding in Primary Raynaud's phenomenon is the absence of any underlying disease or abnormal laboratory findings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Secondary Raynaud's phenomenon: This occurs as a result of an underlying condition or disease, such as autoimmune disorders (e.g., systemic sclerosis, lupus), connective tissue diseases, arterial diseases, or occupational exposure to certain chemicals or vibrations. In Secondary Raynaud's phenomenon, the episodes are often more severe and may be associated with tissue damage or ulceration. Patients may also have abnormal laboratory findings or other signs and symptoms related to an underlying autoimmune disorder."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C & D (Cold sores & Sclerodema) are incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Given the absence of any comorbidities or underlying diseases, the association of symptoms with cold temperatures or stress, and the characteristic color changes during episodes, the most likely diagnosis in this case is Primary Raynaud's phenomenon."}],"qi":[9095]},{"n":44,"q":"Identify the deformity depicted in the image:","o":[{"t":"Swan neck deformity"},{"t":"Boutonniere deformity"},{"t":"Z line deformity"},{"t":"Trigger finger"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Boutonniere deformity:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This deformity involves flexion of the proximal interphalangeal joint and hyperextension of the distal interphalangeal joint of the finger, resulting in a finger with a bent appearance at the middle joint and extended at the end joint."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[9100]},{"n":45,"q":"A patient presents with flaccid bullous lesions as shown in the image below involving the entire trunk and oral cavity. Acantholytic cells are seen on Tzank smear. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pemphigus foliaceus"},{"t":"Pemphigus vulgaris"},{"t":"Dermatitis herpetiformis"},{"t":"Bullous pemphigoid"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pemphigus vulgaris:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the provided information, if the patient is presenting with flaccid bullous lesions involving the entire trunk and oral cavity, along with the presence of acantholytic cells on Tzanck smear, the most probable diagnosis would be Pemphigus vulgaris."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pemphigus vulgaris is known to involve both the skin and mucous membranes, including the oral cavity. The presence of acantholytic cells in Tzanck smear is a characteristic finding in pemphigus vulgaris. However, a definitive diagnosis would require additional clinical evaluation, including a biopsy and direct immunofluorescence testing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pemphigus foliaceus: Althougus pemphigus foliaceous may present with similar features it does not classically involve the mucous membranes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Dermatitis herpetiformis: Dermatitis herpetiformis is a chronic, intensely itchy skin condition that is associated with celiac disease. It is characterized by clusters of small, intensely itchy vesicles and papules on the extensor surfaces of the elbows, knees, and buttocks. It is caused by an autoimmune reaction to gluten. A Tzanck smear is not typically used to diagnose dermatitis herpetiformis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bullous pemphigoid: Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune blistering disorder that primarily affects the elderly. It is characterized by tense bullae that typically occur on the flexural surfaces of the body, such as the armpits and groin. A Tzanck smear is not typically used to diagnose bullous pemphigoid."}],"qi":[9103]},{"n":46,"q":"What condition is likely in a patient who presents to the hospital with symptoms of lethargy, increased sleep, and weight gain, and exhibits low plasma TSH concentration, low T3, and low T4 levels, but shows an increase in TSH levels after the administration of TRH?","o":[{"t":"Hyperthyroidism due to disease in the pituitary"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism due to disease in the pituitary"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient presents with lethargy, increased sleep, and weight gain. These are classic symptoms of hypothyroidism, which is characterized by an underactive thyroid gland. The plasma TSH concentration is low, indicating a lack of stimulation to the thyroid gland. Additionally, both T3 and T4 levels are low, which are the thyroid hormones responsible for regulating metabolism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone) is typically administered to evaluate the function of the hypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid (HPT) axis. In this case, the administration of TRH results in an increase in TSH levels. This indicates that the pituitary gland is capable of responding to TRH by releasing TSH. This suggests a problem with the hypothalamus, which is responsible for producing TRH. Based on the given information, the most likely condition in this patient is Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":47,"q":"The image shown below depicts a specific physical finding. Which of the following conditions is not associated with this finding?","o":[{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Pregnancy"},{"t":"Liver cirrhosis"},{"t":"Hypoestrogenic state"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Hypoestrogenic state:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a hypoestrogenic state, such as menopause or certain medical conditions, the levels of estrogen in the body are reduced. Red hands or palmar erythema is not typically associated with a hypoestrogenic state."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Palmar erythema is more commonly seen in conditions related to increased estrogen levels, such as pregnancy or hormonal disorders. In a hypoestrogenic state, the blood vessels in the skin may not be dilated as much, leading to a normal or pale appearance of the hands rather than redness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are associated with palmar erythema."}],"qi":[9110]},{"n":48,"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has been treated with methotrexate and low-dose corticosteroids for the past 4 months. However, the disease is still progressing. What would be your recommendation for the further management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Stop oral methotrexate and start parenteral methotrexate"},{"t":"Add sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Continue corticosteroids and methotrexate"},{"t":"Start only anti-TNF alpha agents"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Add sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When a patient with rheumatoid arthritis shows inadequate response to initial therapy with methotrexate and low-dose corticosteroids, the addition of other disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) is often recommended. Sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine are commonly used DMARDs and can be added to the treatment regimen to improve disease control."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"9092":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9095":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9066":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9100":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9069":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9103":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9073":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9110":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9082":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});